WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.498
We built the whole transaction manager.

00:00:04.498 --> 00:00:06.997
Locks.

00:00:06.997 --> 00:00:09.495
Schedules.

00:00:09.495 --> 00:00:11.994
Write-ahead logging.

00:00:11.994 --> 00:00:14.492
Crash recovery.

00:00:14.492 --> 00:00:16.991
Strict two phase locking gives you safe, correct concurrency, and it scales to where most production databases run today.

00:00:16.991 --> 00:00:19.489
The next hundred-times leap comes from three protocols that drop it entirely.

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:21.988
Micro-locking.

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:24.486
LSM trees.

00:00:24.486 --> 00:00:26.985
Timestamp ordering.

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:30.041
Think of correctness as nested rings.

00:00:30.041 --> 00:00:33.096
Serializable schedules on the outside.

00:00:33.096 --> 00:00:36.152
Conflict-serializable in the middle.

00:00:36.152 --> 00:00:39.208
Two phase locking inside that.

00:00:39.208 --> 00:00:42.264
Strict two phase locking at the very core.

00:00:42.264 --> 00:00:45.319
Strict two phase locking is the small ring.

00:00:45.319 --> 00:00:48.375
Every ring outside it is a set of schedules that are perfectly safe to run.

00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:51.431
Your database refuses to run them anyway.

00:00:51.431 --> 00:00:54.486
That refused space is the headroom.

00:00:54.486 --> 00:00:57.542
The whole game is finding a protocol that admits more of it.

00:00:57.542 --> 00:01:00.929
Now match a protocol to the work.

00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:04.315
A video goes viral, and ten million users hit one counter.

00:01:04.315 --> 00:01:07.702
That is fine-grained contention.

00:01:07.702 --> 00:01:11.088
Reach for micro-locking.

00:01:11.088 --> 00:01:14.475
Lock the single field, not the whole row, so thousands of writes to one entity barely wait.

00:01:14.475 --> 00:01:17.861
A song lands ten million plays in an hour.

00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:21.248
That is pure write throughput.

00:01:21.248 --> 00:01:24.634
Reach for LSM trees.

00:01:24.634 --> 00:01:28.021
Append every write to an immutable file, no locks at all, and the reads settle a moment later.

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:31.407
Ten thousand agents update one shared graph.

00:01:31.407 --> 00:01:34.794
That is global ordering.

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:38.180
Reach for timestamp ordering.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:41.567
Run every operation in timestamp order, and a conflict aborts and retries instead of waiting.

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:46.321
Strict two phase locking is the baseline.

00:01:46.321 --> 00:01:51.075
The next hundred-times leap lives in the schedules it refuses.

00:01:51.075 --> 00:01:55.830
And the target itself is wider than two phase locking enforces.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:00.584
What defines correct is serializability, equal to some serial order.

00:02:00.584 --> 00:02:05.338
Conflict serializability is only the test two phase locking passes, so a scheduler that admits the serializable but not conflict-serializable schedules is still correct.

00:02:05.338 --> 00:02:10.093
That gap is the headroom.

00:02:10.093 --> 00:02:14.847
Every protocol here ran on one machine.

00:02:14.847 --> 00:02:19.601
Next we break that machine into a thousand, where the data, the locks, and the clock live on different computers.
